  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain showers and isolated thunderstorms arrives \ntonight and through Sunday.\n\nA plume of subtropical moisture has reached the western Great lakes \nthis morning, and will spread eastward across our region tonight \nbringing the next round of rainshowers. This plume of moisture will \nincrease PWats over our region to around 1.5 to 1.75 inches, or \ngreater than the 90th percentile for Buffalo sounding climatology \nfor mid September.  \n\nBroad scale lift ahead of a few shortwave troughs aloft combined \nwith lower level isentropic lift will generate a period of rain \nshowers tonight through Sunday. Activity will advance northeastward \nacross the CWA with light stratiform rain possible across the North \nCountry by daybreak Sunday morning.   \n\nThe axis of deeper instability will remain south of a frontal \nboundary that will remain draped across the midwest, leaving the \nthunder threat low. However the deepening warm cloud depth to over \n12K feet on Sunday combined with the anomalously high moisture \ncontent of the airmass will lend to heavy rain as the primary \nhazard. Basin average QPF will range from around an inch near the \nstate line, to a third/half an inch across the North Country. With \nthe rain showers rather stratiform, falling over a greater duration \nof time the flood threat will remain low. If any convection were to \ndevelop across our southern zones, localized heavier amounts of \nrainfall would be possible. \n\nThese rain showers will shift to the east Sunday afternoon and \nevening, though a moisture rich lower atmosphere will remain Sunday \nand into the evening hours with higher terrain low stratus and fog \npossible. \n\nA broad area of surface high pressure over the province of Ontario \nwill advect a cooler and drier airmass southward later Sunday night \nand into Monday, possibly bringing a brief period of fair weather \nbefore another impulse returns the chances for showers and possible \nthunderstorms for Tuesday and Wednesday.",
